A session in web applications (including Spring) is a way to maintain state between multiple HTTP requests from the same client. 
Since HTTP is stateless by default, the server needs a mechanism to remember the user’s data across requests—this is where sessions 
come in.

✅ What is a Session?

A session is a server-side storage of information about the user.
It is identified by a session ID, usually stored in a cookie (JSESSIONID in Java apps).
When the client sends subsequent requests, the session ID is sent back, allowing the server to retrieve the stored data.


✅ Why do we need sessions?

To keep track of logged-in users.
To store temporary data like:

Authentication details
Shopping cart items
User preferences




✅ How it works in Spring

When a user first accesses the app, the server creates a session and generates a JSESSIONID.
The server sends this ID to the client in the Set-Cookie header.
The client stores it and sends it back in subsequent requests.
The server uses this ID to retrieve the correct session data
Spring creates an HttpSession.
Stores the SecurityContext (which contains Authentication) inside the session.
This allows the user to remain authenticated across multiple requests without re-entering credentials.


✅ Session Lifecycle

Created when the user first interacts (or logs in).
Maintained until timeout or logout.
Destroyed when:

Timeout occurs
User logs out
Server invalidates it


✅ Session Timeout
Configure in application.properties:
Plain Textproperties isn’t fully supported. Syntax highlighting is based on Plain Text.server.servlet.session.timeout=30mShow more lines

✅ Session in Spring Security

By default, Spring Security uses sessions for authentication.
You can control session behavior:

Javahttp.sessionManagement()    .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.IF_REQUIRED);Show more lines
Options:

STATELESS → No session (used for JWT-based APIs).
IF_REQUIRED → Create session only if needed (default).
ALWAYS → Always create session.
NEVER → Never create session.

